Wrong, because you accidentally pasted your code for the "Add Binary" problem instead of "Greatest Common Divisor of Strings".

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: Your code is actually a fantastic, working solution for adding two binary strings. You handled the carry variable perfectly, navigated the strings from right to left safely, and appended the characters cleanly before reversing.

Is it correct: For Greatest Common Divisor of Strings, it is completely incorrect. A failing test case would be str1 = "ABCABC", str2 = "ABC". Your code tries to do math on the characters by subtracting '0', which produces massive random integer values for letters, and returns a binary string instead of a repeating substring.

The fix: The logic needs to be completely replaced.

Logical mistakes: You just mixed up your files or clipboard.

Time and space complexity of MY code: Time is O(max(N, M)) and Space is O(max(N, M)) where N and M are the lengths of strings a and b, because you loop through both and build a new string of their combined length.

Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: It doesn't work for this problem because finding a common repeating substring is a pattern-matching and string-length math problem, not a binary arithmetic problem.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Prefix Checking (Brute Force)

Where it comes from: The most intuitive manual approach, often the first step in logic building.

Idea: Find the shortest of the two strings. Try using the whole shortest string as the repeating pattern. If it doesn't cleanly build both str1 and str2 by repeating itself, chop one letter off the end and try again until you find a match or run out of letters.

C++ code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    bool valid(string str1, string str2, int k) {
        if (str1.length() % k != 0 || str2.length() % k != 0) {
            return false;
        }
        string base = str1.substr(0, k);
        string build1 = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < str1.length() / k; i++) {
            build1 += base;
        }
        string build2 = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < str2.length() / k; i++) {
            build2 += base;
        }
        return build1 == str1 && build2 == str2;
    }

public:
    string gcdOfStrings(string str1, string str2) {
        int len1 = str1.length();
        int len2 = str2.length();
        for (int i = min(len1, len2); i > 0; i--) {
            if (valid(str1, str2, i)) {
                return str1.substr(0, i);
            }
        }
        return "";
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(min(N, M) * (N + M)) because we might test every prefix, and building the test strings takes linear time. Space is O(N + M) to create the temporary test strings.

What makes the next approach better: We don't actually need to test multiple prefixes. We can use math to instantly find the exact length the answer should be, and string concatenation to instantly verify if an answer even exists.

Approach 2: Math and String Concatenation (Optimal)

Where it comes from: The LeetCode editorial and standard mathematical string theory.

Idea: If a common repeating string exists, then gluing str1 to str2 must look exactly the same as gluing str2 to str1. If they match, the length of the greatest common divisor string is exactly the greatest common divisor of their lengths. We just calculate that GCD and slice the string.

